add('lem-finite-normal-surface-cover-completed-local-degree-bound','Completed local degrees of finite normal surface covers',['lem-surface-finite-completion-factors','lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality','thm-completion-preserves-regular-local-rings','lem-normal-domain-implies-s-two','cor-every-system-of-parameters-is-regular-in-a-cohen-macaulay-module','thm-auslander-buchsbaum-formula','cor-equicharacteristic-complete-local-power-series-quotient'],r'''Let $X\to Y$ be finite dominant of degree $n$ between integral normal surfaces in the permitted class, with $Y$ regular. At a closed $x\in X$ over $y\in Y$, the complete normal local domain $\widehat{O_{X,x}}$ is finite over the complete regular local ring $\widehat{O_{Y,y}}$, and its fraction-field degree is at most $n$. In the equicharacteristic setting the latter ring is a power-series ring in two variables over its residue field.''',r'''Put T=O_Y,y and let C be the finite semilocal normal algebra of the cover over Spec T. It is torsion-free of generic rank n. At each maximal ideal q of C its local ring is a normal surface, hence CM of dimension two. A parameter pair of T generates an ideal primary for each such maximal ideal because C is finite over T, so it is a regular sequence on every C_q, and therefore on C. Thus depth_T C=2 and Auslander–Buchsbaum makes C finite free of rank n over T. Finite completion factors identify C⊗T That with the finite product of complete local rings Chat_q. Completion normality makes each nonzero factor a normal local domain; their base maps are finite local and the parameter pair remains regular, so each factor is finite free over the regular That, of some positive rank n_q. The ranks sum to n; localizing at Frac(That) turns each domain factor into its fraction field, of degree n_q≤n. Regularity and dimension two of That follow from completion-preserved regularity and unchanged embedding dimension; the equicharacteristic coefficient-field presentation gives a surjection κ(y)[[u,v]]→That. Its source and target are regular local domains of dimension two, so its prime kernel has height zero and is zero, making it an isomorphism. No global degree equality after splitting into local completion factors is assumed.''')
